@ukdas, @garybernhardt thank you for your feedback!

@ukdas I understand that you have 1000+ cases across ~180 files and you run them in 6 shards. Do you use --fully-parallel more when you run locally? Do you have `beforeAll` hooks in these files / describes?

@garybernhardt your tweet hints that you have a generated file with 700 tests. Does this file contain `beforeAll` hooks? I assume you do, otherwise you would generate multiple files.

One of the reasons we 'simplified' sharding was to give users better control over the parallelism overhead. When distributing the load between the shards, we don't know how expensive it is to shard the file that has `beforeAll` in it. Per-file sharding allows advanced users achieve predictable sharding overhead via balancing their files.

Our assumption was that the typical sharding user operates big numbers of files and that the change won't be even noticed. At a smaller scale, one slow test compromises the shard efficiency the same way. Your feedback is a good signal for us to reconsider this assumption.

@pavelfeldman No, no codegen for the suite in question. This is a single test file with just a `for` loop that loops over every lesson in [Execute Program](https://www.executeprogram.com)'s course catalog, defining two tests per lesson to verify that all lessons actually work. There's no `beforeAll`. It looks like this:

```typescript
test.describe('Course lessons', () => {
  test.beforeEach(() => test.slow())

  for (const course of curriculum.releasedCourses) {
    for (const lesson of course.releasedLessons) {
      test(`Lesson: ${course.id}/${lesson.id}`, async ({page}) => {
        /* Test body goes here. */
      })
    }
  }
})
```

Until this change in Playwright, we parallelized the tests in this file across 20 VMs. With the change, we could theoretically have 20 nearly-identical files, each defining 1/20th of the tests by using `%` or something. But that's very tedious and awkward, especially when it worked perfectly prior to this change.

Understood about the sharding cost of `beforeAll`; I can see that that makes sense for most test suites. For this particular suite it doesn't help at all unfortunately; it only hurts. Just to reiterate, a "parallelize this file at the per-test level" flag would make this problem go away!
